---
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro'
import { contact, roleLine, vcardCompact } from '../lib/contact'
import { qr } from '../lib/qr'

/*
 * A short URL to point a camera at.
 *
 * The code carries the contact card itself rather than a link to it, so a phone
 * can add the entry with the network off. The alternative was to encode
 * https://mosthofaimran.com/contact.vcf, which makes a much smaller code and a
 * much easier scan, and then does nothing at all in a room with no signal. The
 * card is 330 bytes and fits in a 69 module code, which is comfortable to read
 * off a screen, so the offline version wins.
 *
 * The download stays on the page underneath for anyone reading this on the
 * device they would be scanning with.
 */
const payload = vcardCompact()
const code = qr(payload, `Contact card for ${contact.name}, ${roleLine}`)

/*
 * The regex lives here rather than in the markup. Written inline as
 * {contact.code.replace(...)} the slashes inside it confuse the compiler.
 */
const codeUrl = contact.code
const codeHost = contact.code.replace(/^https?:\/\//, '')
---
<Doc
  title="Scan"
  description={`A QR code carrying the contact card for ${contact.name}, ${roleLine}. Scanning it adds the entry without a download and without a network connection.`}>

  <div class="mast">
    <div>Off the Bit
<b>Scan</b>
Category: Contact
Carries: vCard 4.0, {code.payloadBytes} bytes</div>
    <div class="r"><b>M. Imran</b><br />{roleLine}
Index: <a href="/">draft</a>
Section: <a href="/#s14">14</a></div>
  </div>

  <div class="title">
    <h1>Scan</h1>
    <div class="sub">Point a camera at this. It carries the card, not a link to the card, so it works with the network off.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="ind">
    <figure class="qr">
      <div class="qrbox" set:html={code.svg} />
      <figcaption>
        <b>{contact.name}</b><br />
        {roleLine}<br />
        {contact.city}, {contact.country}
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="ind">
    <div class="memo">Most phone cameras read this without an app: open the camera, hold it steady,
      and the contact appears as a prompt. The code contains a vCard 4.0 record with the name, role,
      organisation, mobile number, email, website and city. Nothing is fetched, so nothing is
      logged and it works in a basement.</div>
  </div>

  <!--
    A list rather than a table, and that is not a style choice.

    Astro 5.18.2 duplicates an <a> that sits inside a <table> when both its href
    and its text are expressions: the row renders correctly and a second, empty
    copy of the anchor is hoisted out past the closing tag. Two of those failed
    check-a11y on link-name and on H91.NoContent, in both colour schemes.

    Reduced to a four row page to confirm the boundary. Expression href with
    literal text is fine; expression href with expression text is not. Outside a
    table the same anchor is fine, which is why this list works and why Section
    14 keeps its table: every href in Section 14 is a literal.

    The alternative was to hardcode these two addresses, which would have put a
    fresh copy of a contact fact on the site during the change whose whole
    purpose was removing those copies.
  -->
  <div class="ind">
    <h2 id="instead">If the camera will not read it</h2>
    <ul class="routes">
      <li><b>Download</b><span><a href="/contact.vcf" download>contact.vcf</a>, the full card. Same source as this code, with a revision stamp and a note the code leaves out.</span></li>
      <li><b>Email</b><span><a href={`mailto:${contact.email}`}>{contact.email}</a></span></li>
      <li><b>Code</b><span><a href={codeUrl}>{codeHost}</a></span></li>
      <li><b>Everything else</b><span><a href="/#s14">Section 14, Author's Address</a></span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="ind">
    <h2 id="how">How it is drawn</h2>
    <p class="prose">Encoded during the build and written into this page as vector paths. There is
      no image request, no generator service and no script: the reading path here ships zero bytes
      of JavaScript, and a QR code is one of the few things that can still be produced under that
      constraint without giving anything up.</p>
    <p class="prose dim">Version {code.version}, {code.size} by {code.size} modules, error correction
      level M. The white background is painted by the drawing rather than inherited from the page,
      in both colour schemes, because an inverted code is out of specification and older scanners
      return nothing when they meet one. The short address is mosthofaimran.com/scan.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="foot"><span>Imran</span><span>Contact</span><span>[Scan]</span></div>
</Doc>

<style>
  /*
   * The cap is set from the module count, not from taste.
   *
   * At 69 modules 320px was comfortable. Carrying the note took the code to 85,
   * and holding the cap would have shrunk every module by a fifth, which is
   * exactly the thing that makes a code fail on a phone held at an angle in a
   * dim room. 380px keeps a module the same physical size as before. On a
   * narrow screen the figure is bounded by the column anyway.
   */
  .qr {
    margin: 22px 0 0;
    max-width: 380px;
  }
  .qrbox {
    /* The quiet zone is inside the SVG, so the padding here is only visual
       separation from the page, not part of the code. */
    background: #ffffff;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 2px;
    padding: 10px;
    line-height: 0;
  }
  .qr figcaption {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--dim);
    margin-top: 10px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }
  .qr figcaption b {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: 13.5px;
  }

  /* Shaped like the tables elsewhere in the document: a label column, a rule
     between rows, and the value wrapping under the label on a narrow screen. */
  .routes {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 14px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .routes li {
    display: flex;
    gap: 16px;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 10px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-family: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--fs-prose);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .routes b {
    flex: none;
    width: 150px;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .routes span { max-width: 62ch; }
  @media screen and (max-width: 560px) {
    .routes li { display: block; }
    .routes b { display: block; width: auto; margin-bottom: 3px; }
  }

  /* Printed on a card or a slide, the code should be the whole point of the
     page and the surrounding argument should get out of the way. */
  @media print {
    .qr { max-width: 260px; }
    .qrbox { border: 0; padding: 0; }
  }
</style>
